<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Park!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Connect ideas from a text with personal experiences. Concept: Show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can connect ideas from a text with personal experiences by pointing to images (e.g., points to a market picture and says, "I go there on the weekend."). Can show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences by drawing familiar places (e.g., draw a park to show "my favorite place"). Can relate new vocabulary to familiar places by pointing and naming (e.g., points to "school" and says "I go to school.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher displays large flashcards or realia of community places (e.g., a small toy bus, a picture of a park, a school). Teacher points to each and asks, "What is this?" encouraging choral response: "It's a park!", "It's a school!" Then, teacher asks, "Do you go there?" and models pointing to themselves and saying, "Yes, I go to the park!" or "No, I don't go to the library." Students respond by pointing and saying "Yes, I go!" or "No." This connects classroom vocabulary to their personal experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher shows a simple drawing of a 'route' from a home to a park on the board. Teacher says, "This is my home," pointing to the home. Then, "I walk to the street, then I see the bus. I go to the park. The park is big. I play there." Teacher points to each place as named, modeling the connection between the drawing and the description. Teacher emphasizes /p/ in "park" and /m/ in "market" clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the park on the drawing) "Is this a school?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the drawing of a child playing) "Do I play at the market?" (Expected answer: "No, play at the park!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> Teacher presents a large, colorful, simple "community map" poster with previously learned places: park, market, school, library, church, home, playground, bus stop (street, road, building). The map has simple lines indicating routes. Each place is clearly labeled with a picture and the word. Teacher also has small individual student handouts with a blank "My Community Map" to start drawing on later.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated map poster (genre: informational map; register: simple, descriptive; format: visual, labeled diagram). Blank "My Community Map" handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing places and activities: "The park is big. I play there." Answering questions: "Where is the library?" "Next to the market." Relate new vocabulary to familiar places by pointing and naming.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher points to the map poster. "What is this? Is it a house?" (Expected answer: "No, it's a map!") "What places do you see?" (Students point and name: "Park!", "Market!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (TPR):</b> Teacher makes a statement while pointing to the map. "The school is small." If true, students show thumbs up. If false, thumbs down. (e.g., "The park is big." "The market is next to the church.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Connecting ideas from a text (map labels) with personal experiences, showing connections between concepts and personal experiences. Using WH-questions ("Where is it?"), Present simple ("It's the market."), Adjectives + nouns ("big park"), and prepositions (in, on, next to, near). Practicing /p/, /m/, /ʈʃ/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice: "Map Match & Say"</b> Teacher distributes small picture cards of community places (park, market, school, church, home). Students match each card to the corresponding place on the large classroom map poster, pointing and saying, "This is the park," or "This is my home." Teacher guides: "Where is the church?" (Students point and say, "It's the church!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled: "Describe My Route"</b> Teacher projects a simple, animated route on the map poster (e.g., from 'home' to 'park'). Teacher asks, "Where do I go first?" "What is next to the park?" Students use prepositions and familiar vocabulary (e.g., "Street," "Next to the market."). Teacher asks students to trace the route with their finger on the projected image and say, "Go to the street, then to the park." Emphasize polite requests if asking for directions: "Excuse me, where is the park?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Connect My Map"</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a pre-drawn, simple community map handout (different from their project map, simpler) with 3-4 key places. They take turns explaining their map to their partner by pointing and naming places: "This is my school. It's big." "The market is near the church." Their partner listens and points to the named place on their own map if it's there, or asks "Where is the market?" This practice helps students connect their visual representation (map) to spoken language.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual presentation and explanation of "My Community Map" (Project 1). Students will draw and label their own community map and then orally explain it to a partner or a small group, demonstrating connection between their drawing and their personal experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take out their "My Community Map" handouts from Stage 2. Teacher instructs them to draw 3-5 important places from their own community (e.g., home, park, school, market, church) and label them. They also draw routes or streets connecting these places. (e.g., a "big park", "small store", "busy street").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then models how to present their map: "This is my home. This is the big park. I go to the park from my home. The school is next to the market."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn pointing to places on their own map and explaining them to their group members. They relate the drawn places to their personal experiences by saying things like, "I play in the park!" or "My mom goes to the market on the weekend." They also point and say, "Where is the church? It's near the park."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members listen and ask simple WH-questions about the map or respond, "Oh, a big park!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Community Map" presentations, focusing on student's ability to connect ideas and use target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Community Map" to the teacher or a peer, pointing to drawn places and verbally connecting them to personal experiences or using descriptive language. For example, "This is my school. It is big. I go there every day." or "My market is busy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target words (park, market, church) clearly, specifically /p/, /m/, /ʈʃ/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students name at least 3-4 community places and use 1-2 adjectives (e.g., big, small) or prepositions (e.g., next to, near).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Connection:</b> Students clearly point to places on their map and make a simple connection to a personal experience (e.g., "I play here," "I go there.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students listen respectfully to peers and ask simple, polite questions if prompted (e.g., "Excuse me, where is the library?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a star next to their favorite place on their community map. Below the map, they complete the sentence: "My favorite place is _______." (They write or draw the place and say it to the teacher as they leave).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new places did you draw on your map today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did you like about explaining your map to your friends?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Overall, students demonstrated good understanding of community places. The "Community Map" project was highly engaging for connecting concepts to personal experiences. Most students were able to point and name places, and many used simple descriptive adjectives like "big" or "small." Some students needed more prompting for prepositions like "next to" or "near." The mediation skill of connecting the map to their real life was successful through simple utterances and pointing. Adjustments for next time could include providing more sentence frames during map explanation, such as "I see the ____. It is ____." to encourage more complete sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Exploring My Community" theme. The maps can be displayed, serving as a visual anchor for future vocabulary recall. The next theme will build on descriptive language and identifying places, possibly focusing on describing people in the community or activities in different places.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>